{"commit": "44e14b15e51daaf010a554be07ee60ea95522c8d", "id": "44e14b15e51daaf010a554be07ee60ea95522c8d-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Proposal names no upstream test", "repo": "https://github.com/PSeitz/lz4_flex", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T001948Z-317243fb", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/44e14b15e51daaf010a554be07ee60ea95522c8d-1/20260924T001948Z-317243fb/origin.json": "896b215d0055ec772bfc8f3af5a776dd4fecaca9f57b1e176d33cfb919a7779f", "evidence/44e14b15e51daaf010a554be07ee60ea95522c8d-1/20260924T001948Z-317243fb/proposal.json": "8e6ec22cb6ff475bdd1a478a751b44e7d0291d863a29a19bb1e76a79b700de3e"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "lz4_flex", "status": "blocked", "subject": "Don't panic on drop", "summary": "`AutoFinishEncoder::drop` called `panic!` when its implicit `try_finish()` returned an error from the underlying writer. Dropping the encoder while a writer was failing therefore aborted the program \u2014 an unrecoverable surprise for callers who chose `auto_finish` precisely so they wouldn't have to handle finish errors. The fix swallows the result with `let _ = encoder.try_finish();`.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:19:48.857131+00:00"}
{"commit": "e0e7e5c223441c7739c9a140c1e62db0399621ba", "id": "e0e7e5c223441c7739c9a140c1e62db0399621ba-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Proposal names no upstream test", "repo": "https://github.com/PSeitz/lz4_flex", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T002006Z-00ae09b0", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/e0e7e5c223441c7739c9a140c1e62db0399621ba-1/20260924T002006Z-00ae09b0/origin.json": "22d0ec3137c1c03edf0341f9f80047a436e6f3d346898b43be25fb69788bf850", "evidence/e0e7e5c223441c7739c9a140c1e62db0399621ba-1/20260924T002006Z-00ae09b0/proposal.json": "88954a8b8615ee73606aad195f0ac0dcdb7bae31feb0bb3ecd7becf9bcf51603"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "lz4_flex", "status": "blocked", "subject": "Don't panic passing an empty buffer to decompress_size_prepended", "summary": "`decompress_size_prepended` directly indexed `input[0..=3]` to recover the prepended uncompressed size. Calling it with an input shorter than 4 bytes (in particular the empty slice) panicked with 'index out of bounds: the len is 0 but the index is 0' before any decompression code ran. The fix routes the read through a `super::uncompressed_size` helper that returns `Err(ExpectedAnotherByte)` when the input is too short.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:20:06.895534+00:00"}
